3 Ways to Manage Users:
1. Internal User Management (Default)
Use the built-in API to manage users:
bash# Create a user
curl -X POST http://localhost:8080/api/v1/users \
  -H "X-API-Key: ak_test_key_12345" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "id": "emp001",
    "username": "Alice Johnson",
    "email": "alice@company.com",
    "role": "employee",
    "metadata": {
      "department": "Engineering",
      "employeeId": "EMP-12345"
    }
  }'

# List all users
curl http://localhost:8080/api/v1/users \
  -H "X-API-Key: ak_test_key_12345"

# Update user
curl -X PUT http://localhost:8080/api/v1/users/emp001 \
  -H "X-API-Key: ak_test_key_12345" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"role": "manager"}'
2. Bulk Sync from External System
Import users from your existing system:
bashcurl -X POST http://localhost:8080/api/v1/users/sync \
  -H "X-API-Key: ak_test_key_12345" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "users": [
      {
        "id": "emp001",
        "username": "Alice",
        "email": "alice@company.com",
        "role": "employee"
      },
      {
        "id": "emp002",
        "username": "Bob",
        "email": "bob@company.com",
        "role": "manager"
      }
    ]
  }'
3. Configure External Authentication
Point to your existing authentication system:
bash# Configure to use external API
curl -X POST http://localhost:8080/api/v1/auth/config \
  -H "X-API-Key: ak_test_key_12345" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "type": "api",
    "endpoint": "https://your-auth-system.com/api/users",
    "apiKey": "your-auth-system-api-key",
    "enabled": true
  }'

# Or configure JWT validation
curl -X POST http://localhost:8080/api/v1/auth/config \
  -H "X-API-Key: ak_test_key_12345" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "type": "jwt",
    "enabled": true
  }'
